Communication Models ====
- process is either: linear, interactive, transactional
- factors: sender, receiver, channel

Linear Model ===
- most simplistic
- message is moved down a line until it reaches intended audience (like telephone?)
- factors and steps:
    1) sender (info source) 
    2) encodes msg (taking abstract thoughts and turns them into a msg)
    3) msg (the content ^)
    4) channel (the mdeium of communication -- phone/email/tv)
    5) receiver (the intended audience)
    6) decodes msg (interprets msg content to try to understand sender)     
    7) noise (interference w transmission of the msg -- context/cultural differences -- anything that might make it hard for sender and receiver to understand each other)
- so it's: 
sender -> channel -> noise -> receiver

Interactive Model ===
- receiver is expected to respond to the sender's msg, and the linear model starts again
- so it's:
sender -> channel -> noise -> receiver
receiver <- noise <- channel <- sender

Transactional Model ===
- speaker and listener are simulataneously encoding and decoding msgs w/in an environment that includes the context of the situation with the noise
- this model gives the most complex view of comms

noise can be 
1) environmental (construction/traffic)
2) physiological (sick audience member)
3) psychological (studying for a test in the next class)


Communication Mediums ===
- theory of media richness: advances the notion that communication richness/leaness is an objective property of communication media
- media richness: ability to facilitate shared understanding w/in a time interval
    - how mediums can enhance/detract from an intended msg/meaning
    - encourage/discourrage two-way communication
    - face-to-face com might be a richer media than emailing
- media richness determine dby:
    - capacity for immediate feedback
    - capcity to transmit multiple cues
    - language variety
    - capcity of the medium to have a perosnal focus (expression of emotions/feelings by all communicative partners)


Presentational Speaking ===
- presentational speaking: a form of communication that is used in daily interactions
- less formal than public speaking
- public speaking doesn't allow for in-time audience feedback
